12:47:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You've hit on a critical layer that I should have emphasized earlier. Yes, **loss of public trust** is the deeper, often irreversible factor that makes redenomination fail—and it's distinct from inflation itself. ## Inflation vs. Trust: The Two Layers - **Inflation** is a monetary phenomenon: too much money chasing too few goods. It can be stopped by credible monetary policy (e.g., an independent central bank, fiscal discipline). - **Trust** is a psychological and institutional phenomenon: people's belief that the currency will hold value *tomorrow*. Once trust is destroyed, no amount of zero-removal or technical fixes can restore it quickly. Venezuela's case is a perfect illustration: after three redenominations, people no longer believe any version of the bolívar will be stable. They mentally discount the new notes as "just a temporary game." As a result: - Even if inflation were magically stopped, people would still prefer dollars, because the government has broken its promise so many times. - The new currency (even after removing zeros) is treated like a worthless token, not a store of value. - Prices are often set in dollars anyway; bolívar prices are updated hourly or daily to keep up with the real exchange rate. ## Why Trust Makes Redenomination Useless When trust is gone, the new notes are seen as **the same old bolívar with a different name**. People immediately convert them into goods, dollars, or other assets—which itself drives inflation further. The redenomination becomes a self-fulfilling disaster: 1. Government announces new currency. 2. Everyone expects it to fail (based on past experience). 3. They rush to spend old notes (if still accepted) or reject new notes. 4. Prices skyrocket, validating the expectation. This is exactly what happened in Venezuela: each redenomination was met with a wave of panic spending and dollarization, not renewed confidence. ## The Real World Lesson Countries that successfully removed zeros (e.g., Turkey, Brazil, Poland) did so **only after** inflation was already low and stable, and the central bank had built credibility over years. They didn't use redenomination to *create* trust—they used it *after* trust was restored, simply to simplify accounting. In contrast, countries like Venezuela, Zimbabwe, and Yugoslavia tried to use redenomination as a shortcut to restore value. It never worked, because you can't print trust. You can issue new notes, but you can't force people to believe in them. ## Your Point in Summary Yes, you are absolutely right: Venezuela's failed attempts prove that **redenomination is not just about inflation—it's fundamentally about credibility.** If the public no longer trusts the government or the central bank to maintain the currency's value, removing zeros is like putting new wallpaper on a crumbling house. The house falls anyway. So the answer to your original question becomes even clearer: countries with cheap currencies cannot simply remove zeros because **zero removal doesn't restore the one thing that makes a currency valuable: trust.** Without trust, the zeros are just a cosmetic trick—and the market will instantly punish the illusion.
